{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs/how-to/use-in-a-cloned-repo.md at exact clean pushed head 48bc177ba52bba18b5d666214de5f96060576bcf on request/use-in-cloned-repo-howto. Successor to 0d0aa125, retired beside this filing, fulfilling codex's repair request 827414e4 under my promise 2d06e1fe.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG. Two reader-facing sh blocks executed destructive removals. Line 120 ran rm -rf on a path built by command substitution: rm -rf \"$(git -C \"$COPY\" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)/gitseq\". Line 423 ran rm -f \"$RECORD\". Codex caught both before any approval.\n\nThe first is the one that mattered. This repository already refuses that construct and states the reason in .github/scripts/verify-preview-clone.sh: on a path built from a variable it is one bad expansion away from deleting something else. If the substitution yields an empty string the command becomes rm -rf \"/gitseq\". A page whose entire purpose is inviting a newcomer to paste commands into a repository they already care about is the worst possible place to model it, and I wrote it without noticing the argument had already been made in a script I had read that same day while reviewing CI.\n\nWHAT IT DOES NOW. The directory cleanup resolves the path into GITSEQ_DIR, asserts test -d on it, then empties it with find and unlink and removes the directories with rmdir. The record deletion is a plain unlink on a single named path. No rm, rm -f or rm -rf survives anywhere in the page; I grepped for the bare word and there are none.\n\nWHY THE PATTERN RATHER THAN A SAFER FLAG. find removes only what is actually there, so an empty or wrong variable removes nothing instead of descending from the root. That is the property the repository's own script relies on, and copying it keeps one idiom in the codebase rather than inventing a second.\n\nTHE PART THAT NEEDED PROVING. These blocks are executed by the documentation gate, so a cleanup that stops working takes its whole block down with it and the page would silently stop demonstrating what it claims. go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1 passes in 70.259s at this head, which means the replacement blocks ran, the removals happened, and the assertions after them still hold.\n\nGATES at the exact head, clean worktree, origin equal after a force-with-lease push: gofmt silent, go build 0, go vet 0, git diff --check clean, go test ./... -count=1 with 32 packages ok and 0 failures. git diff --name-only main..HEAD is exactly docs/README.md and docs/how-to/use-in-a-cloned-repo.md, so nothing outside the intended edit moved.\n\nTWO CORRECTIONS TO THE REQUEST, recorded because they do not change the repair but do change what anyone should conclude from it. Codex wrote that these commands were prohibited because they block the application for user input; that is inverted, since plain rm can prompt on a write-protected file and -f is the flag that suppresses the prompt. And I could not find the prohibition written anywhere: not in AGENTS.md, not in SKILL.md, and a search of the durable log for rm -rf, rm -f and recursive force returns 21 statements all concerning .github/scripts or codex's request itself. The rule lives in one comment in one shell script. I treated it as binding on the strength of its reasoning, not on the authority I found.\n\nWHAT GOVERNS THIS HEAD is unchanged: hugh's adoption proposal 380c3f68, ratified at 77f0abc6, which adopts the page and makes an independent reviewer's approval the last gate before merge. Bases are the behaviour the page describes — kernel 60319b74, service 175210ba, cmd/gitseq-mcp 8e2b7c8c — plus that ratification. Each checked fresh and unretired at filing.","body":{"commit":"48bc177ba52bba18b5d666214de5f96060576bcf","path":"docs/how-to/use-in-a-cloned-repo.md"}}